2.text:
In the stationary state
\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot v_{k}\rangle =0\qquad\langle\dot  u_{k}\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
so that on the time scale on which the weights change
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\,\mu_{k}\ ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the quasi static approximation $v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}$,
$u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$, 
\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle=
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho \Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$, $w_{k}\equiv w$ and
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$.  With
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\big[1+c_{kl}{\bf 1}_{k\ne l}\big]$,
$c_{kl}\equiv c\;(k\ne l)$ and $c_{kk}=1$, 
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big\langle x_{k}x_{l}\big\rangle/\mu^{2}
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[\,S_{k}+1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the mean field the stationary condition is
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S_{k}+1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)\Big]+\rho S_{k}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $S_{k}\equiv S$
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu c\,(S-1)=-\,S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)-\phi\mu  .
\end{equation*}
Writing the stationary condition in the usual form
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\rho_{0}\langle u_{k}\rangle$,
$\rho_{0}= -\rho>0$, one obtains the familiar form
\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)= \Big[\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1\Big]S-1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that the correlation which makes $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle=0$ is
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
with, in the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$,
\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]=
-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the minus sign being conventional.  With the usual neurophysiological
sign convention $\rho<0$ for heterosynaptic depression and writing
$\rho_{0}=-\rho>0$, $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$, this is
normally quoted as
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}-1>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu-1$, the dimensionless balance
between the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ and the heterosynaptic offset
$|\rho|$.  Up to the inessential overall sign this is the
$\kappa$ which is usually written as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, and with
the sign convention $\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$ as
$\kappa=\rho_{0}/\phi\mu-1$, so that $c^{*}=[\kappa S-1]/(S-1)$ is the
mean–field clustering threshold.  In particular with the strictly
positive convention $\kappa_{+}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ one has
$c^{*}= \big[\kappa_{+}S-1\big]/(S-1)$ with the $1$ absorbed in the
definition of $\rho$.  This is the form quoted in the literature,
$\kappa\approx \rho/(\phi\mu)$ up to the additive constant $1$ which
is fixed by the self–term $s_{kk}=1$. ∎

12.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be written in the stationary
limit as
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle =c_{kl}\sigma^{2}$,
$\sigma^{2}\propto\mu^{2}$, 
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
=\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big\langle x_{l}x_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\Big[\mu^{2}S+\sigma^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\neq l$, $\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,
\Sigma$, $\Sigma\equiv\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=S-1$, and, up to the common
filter factor $\tau_{v}/\tau_{u}$, 
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle=0\iff
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with $\sigma^{2}=\beta\mu^{2}$,
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\phi\beta\mu^{2}\big[1+c\Sigma\big]+\rho\mu S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. 
\begin{equation*}
c\,\phi\beta\mu^{2}\Sigma=-S\big[\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu\big]-\phi\beta\mu^{2},
\end{equation*}
and therefore with $\Sigma=S-1$
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=-
\frac{S\bigl[\,1+\rho/(\phi\mu)\,\bigr]/\beta+1}{S-1}
=
\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1} ,
\end{equation*}
so that, in the usual normalisation $\beta=1$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle=c_{kl}\mu^{2}$,
\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
=-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the trivial self–term $c_{kk}=1$ the dimensionless
threshold which is usually quoted is
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the self term
kept explicitly in the numerator this is the familiar form
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}= \big[\kappa S-1\big]/(S-1)$, and
$c^{*}\to\kappa$ for a very wide dendritic spread.  The sign
convention is such that for the usual LTD offset $\rho<0$ the
asymptotic threshold is $\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu>0$.  Removing the
conventional $c_{kk}=1$ self contribution this is often written
simply as $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$.

8.text:
In the mean field the two traces can be written in the stationary limit as

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, for a white shot noise, 

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle \delta v_{k}(t)\,\delta u_{k}(t)\big\rangle=
\frac{\phi}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\,
\big\langle \delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\big\rangle \;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
=c_{lk}\mu^{2}\delta(t-t')$ 

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle=
\phi\mu^{2}wS_{k}+\frac{\phi\mu^{2}w}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}
\Big[\,s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big] ,
\qquad S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
The mean field drift is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle =0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $l\ne k$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu S+\rho S+\frac{\phi\mu}{\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}}
\Big[1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the inessential filter prefactor $\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}$ which is
usually absorbed in the definition of $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\mu\,c\,(S-1)=-\,\phi\mu  ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c\big(S-1\big)=\kappa S-1,
\qquad 
\kappa\equiv\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that in the normalised notation

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless BCM–like threshold.  With $\rho<0$, $\kappa<1$ and
$c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\gg1$.  In the convention in which the filter
prefactor is written out explicitly the dimensionless combination which
appears in the mean field equation is 
$\,\kappa_{0}=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$, i.e. up to the
common sign and the inessential factor $\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}$ the
usual form $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ is obtained.
The minus sign is conventionally absorbed in the definition
$\rho\to-\rho$, so that with the sign convention $\dot w\propto u(v-\theta)$
one writes the familiar $\kappa=1-\theta/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ BCM threshold which is often quoted as $\kappa=
1+\rho/\phi\mu$ and, with the filter time constants kept explicit,
$\kappa_{0}=-(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]$, the minus
sign being absorbed in $\rho<0$. ∎

10.text:
For the mean field the filtered pre- and post-synaptic traces can be written
in the steady state as 

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\,\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\,\mu S_{k}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the usual white–noise  approximation for the spike trains,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle 
      =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle .
\end{equation*}
With $\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle =\mu^{2}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]$ for
$l\ne k$ and $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\Bigl[S_{k}+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Bigr] ,
\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle \propto 
     \langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
     =w\mu\Bigl[\,\phi\mu S_{k}+\rho S_{k}
          +\phi\mu\bigl[1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\bigr]\Bigr]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. for the uniform solution $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)+\phi\mu\,
\Bigl[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Bigr]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Dividing by $\phi\mu$ and putting $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]+1+c\,(S-1)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventionally introduced

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the zero growth condition can be written as

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\,S\,\kappa  ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential sign convention

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the usual form being quoted with the depressive offset
$\rho<0$ as $\kappa=1-|\rho|/(\phi\mu)<1$.  In particular with the
convention $\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$ one writes
$\kappa=1-\rho_{0}/(\phi\mu)$.  This is the familiar
$\kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ homeostatic factor, $ \kappa<1$ for a
depressive offset $\rho<0$, which in the limit $S\to\infty$ gives
$c^{*}\to\kappa$.  Up to the sign convention for the offset this is
often written as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  If the offset is taken as a
positive depression $\rho_{0}>0$, $\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$,
the conventionally quoted form is $\kappa_{0}=1-\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ with $\rho=-\rho_{0}$.

11.text:
In the mean field one may put $w_{k}\equiv w,~ \mu_{k}\equiv\mu$ and replace the
filters by their stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k}\;,
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $S\equiv S_{k}$ on a homogeneous branch.
Writing $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\big[\,1+c_{kl}\,\big]$ with
$c_{kk}=1$ the mean Hebbian drive is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[S+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and with $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S-1\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the trivial time–constant prefactor,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi \mu^{2}w\Big[S+1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]
   +\rho w\mu S  ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. after division by $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S+1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the condition in the form $1+c(S-1)=\kappa S$ we obtain

\begin{equation*}
 \kappa S=1+c(S-1) , \qquad 
\kappa =-\,1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar homeostatic ratio.  With the usual sign convention
$\rho<0$ for depression the depressing ratio is often quoted without the
trivial self–term as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}= -\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}<1\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential additive $1$ $ \kappa_{0}\simeq -\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
the ratio of the homeostatic offset to the Hebbian drive.  If the
self–correlation $c_{kk}=1$ is kept explicit this is the
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above, which is conventionally written
as $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ for $|\rho|\gg\phi\mu$.  This is the
ratio $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ which is often quoted in the literature
as the homeostatic parameter $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ . ∎

