$l\ne k$ and $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\Bigl[S_{k}+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Bigr] ,
\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle \propto 
     \langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
     =w\mu\Bigl[\,\phi\mu S_{k}+\rho S_{k}
          +\phi\mu\bigl[1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\bigr]\Bigr]=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. for the uniform solution $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)+\phi\mu\,
\Bigl[\,1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Bigr]=0 .
\end{equation*}
Dividing by $\phi\mu$ and putting $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]+1+c\,(S-1)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventionally introduced

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the zero growth condition can be written as

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\,S\,\kappa  ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential sign convention

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the usual form being quoted with the depressive offset
$\rho<0$ as $\kappa=1-|\rho|/(\phi\mu)<1$.  In particular with the
convention $\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$ one writes
$\kappa=1-\rho_{0}/(\phi\mu)$.  This is the familiar
$\kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ homeostatic factor, $ \kappa<1$ for a
depressive offset $\rho<0$, which in the limit $S\to\infty$ gives
$c^{*}\to\kappa$.  Up to the sign convention for the offset this is
often written as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  If the offset is taken as a
positive depression $\rho_{0}>0$, $\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$,
the conventionally quoted form is $\kappa_{0}=1-\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ with $\rho=-\rho_{0}$.

public_text.11:
In the mean field one may put $w_{k}\equiv w,~ \mu_{k}\equiv\mu$ and replace the
filters by their stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k}\;,
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $S\equiv S_{k}$ on a homogeneous branch.
Writing $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\big[\,1+c_{kl}\,\big]$ with
$c_{kk}=1$ the mean Hebbian drive is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[S+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and with $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S-1\big) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the trivial time–constant prefactor,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi \mu^{2}w\Big[S+1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]
   +\rho w\mu S  ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. after division by $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S+1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the condition in the form $1+c(S-1)=\kappa S$ we obtain

\begin{equation*}
 \kappa S=1+c(S-1) , \qquad 
\kappa =-\,1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar homeostatic ratio.  With the usual sign convention
$\rho<0$ for depression the depressing ratio is often quoted without the
trivial self–term as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}= -\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}<1\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential additive $1$ $ \kappa_{0}\simeq -\rho/\phi\mu>0$,
the ratio of the homeostatic offset to the Hebbian drive.  If the
self–correlation $c_{kk}=1$ is kept explicit this is the
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above, which is conventionally written
as $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ for $|\rho|\gg\phi\mu$.  This is the
ratio $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ which is often quoted in the literature
as the homeostatic parameter $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ . ∎

2.text:
In the quasi static limit $v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}$,
$u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ and with $w_{l}\equiv w$,
$\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k}\;,\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\; ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[S_{k}+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\big[\,1+c_{kl}\,\big]$,
$c_{kk}=1$.  On a homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and $c_{kl}\equiv c$,
$k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to an irrelevant positive prefactor

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle \dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle
= w\mu\Big\{\phi\mu\big[S+1+c(S-1)\big]+\rho S\Big\} .
\end{equation*}
Dividing by $w\mu\phi\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
S+1+c\,(S-1)=-\,S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-\,S\,\kappa_{0},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless BCM–like homeostatic factor.  With the sign
convention $\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ this is usually written
as

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)=\kappa S-1,\qquad 
\kappa=-\kappa_{0}=-\,\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventionally absorbed overall sign,
$\rho\to-\rho_{0},\;\rho_{0}>0$, $\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}\big(v_{k}-
\rho_{0}\big)$, the positive homeostatic factor

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{+}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =1-\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}\;<1\qquad\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
is recovered.  In particular $\kappa_{+}\to1$ for the pure Hebbian
rule $\rho\to0$ and $\kappa_{+}\to0$ for strong heterosynaptic
depression.  Up to the inessential overall sign this is the
$\kappa=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$  which is normally quoted as
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$, and with
$\rho\equiv-\theta$ the familiar form $\kappa=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ is
obtained.  With the sign kept as in $\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ this
is $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$, i.e. the $-\,(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$
form quoted above. ∎

6.text:
In the uniform mean field one can put $w_{k}\equiv w$, $\mu _{k}\equiv \mu $
and on the time scale on which $w_{k}$ changes

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w \mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the quasi static approximation $v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}$,

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
Now

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\,1+c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
On a homogeneous branch $S_{k}\equiv S$ and with $c_{kk}=1$,
$c_{kl}=c\;,\;k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that, up to an inessential positive filter prefactor,

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\Big[S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c\,(S-1)=-S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)-\phi\mu ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)=-\,S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the zero growth condition in the conventional form
$1+c\,(S-1)=\kappa S$ one obtains the dimensionless homeostatic
factor

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the inessential overall sign

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the LTD convention $\rho=-\theta<0$,
$\kappa_{0}=1-\theta/\phi\mu<1$.  In the sign convention used in the
statement $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)$ the self term
$c_{kk}=1$ is kept explicit and the zero growth condition is usually
written

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa=-\kappa_{0}$.  The minus sign is the usual
$\rho\to-\theta$ convention change and is inessential; with the
conventional subtractive form $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\big(v_{k}-
\theta\big)$, $\theta=-\rho>0$, this is the familiar
$\kappa=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ BCM–like threshold.  Keeping the filter
overlap explicit the positive prefactor $\propto
\tau_{u}\tau_{v}/(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$ can be absorbed in $\phi$ and the
combination which is often quoted with the time constants written out is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}\propto\Bigl(\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Bigr)
\bigl(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}\bigr) ,
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ in the
$\tau_{v},\tau_{u}\to0$ normalisation.  This is the
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ homeostatic ratio which controls the
clustering threshold, $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$, and with the
sign convention $\dot w\propto u(v-\theta)$ it is quoted as
$\kappa=1-\theta/\phi\mu$.  The overall sign is conventional, the
commonly quoted positive form being $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

