$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is the dimensionless ratio which multiplies
$S$ in $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$; with the literal $+\rho$ sign this is
$\kappa_{s}=-\kappa_{0}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.

Keeping the $DC$ gains of the two first order filters explicit the same
combination is written in dimensional form as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}\propto\Bigl(\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Bigr)
\bigl(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}\bigr)
\propto\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi}\bigl(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v}\bigr),
\end{equation*}
which after the usual normalisation $\int h_{u}h_{v}\propto
(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ absorbed in the definition of $\phi$ reduces to
the dimensionless ratio $\kappa_{0}= (\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu=
1+\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above, i.e. in the $v-\theta$ convention
$\kappa_{0}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$, the $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$
homeostatic factor commonly quoted in the literature.  Up to the trivial
overall sign this is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ BCM threshold with the
signed literal form $\kappa_{s}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.

10.text:
